.\" fclose.3 - close a stream
.TH FCLOSE 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fclose \- close a stream
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, \-lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "int fclose(FILE *" stream );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR fclose ()
function flushes the stream pointed to by
.I stream
(writing any buffered output data using
.BR fflush (3))
and closes the underlying file descriptor.
.PP
Once a stream has been closed, it is removed from the global list of
open streams maintained by the C library, the underlying file
descriptor is released by means of
.BR close (2),
any buffer that was allocated by the library on behalf of the stream
is freed, and the
.B FILE
object itself is deallocated.  Any further use of
.I stream
results in undefined behaviour.
.PP
The behaviour of
.BR fclose ()
is undefined if the
.I stream
parameter is an invalid pointer or has already been closed; the only
input that is explicitly handled is a NULL pointer, which is rejected
as described in RETURN VALUE.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Upon successful completion,
.BR fclose ()
returns 0.  Otherwise, it returns
.B EOF
and may set
.I errno
to indicate the error.
.PP
In particular,
.BR fclose ()
returns
.B EOF
without performing any action when
.I stream
is a NULL pointer.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EBADF
The file descriptor underlying
.I stream
is not valid.
.PP
The
.BR fclose ()
function may also fail and set
.I errno
for any of the errors specified for the routines
.BR close (2),
.BR write (2),
or
.BR fflush (3).
.PP
Note that the Substrate implementation always returns 0 when a non-NULL
.I stream
is supplied, even when the underlying
.BR close (2)
or buffer flush fails; portable code should not rely on this and should
treat any non-zero return as an error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR close (2),
.BR fcloseall (3),
.BR fflush (3),
.BR fopen (3),
.BR setbuf (3)
.SH STANDARDS
.BR fclose ()
conforms to C89, C99, C11, and POSIX.1-2008.
